<Page
  x:Class="VisorVRApp.HelpPage"
  xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
  xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
  xmlns:local="using:VisorVRApp"
  xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/expression/blend/2008"
  xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
  mc:Ignorable="d"
  Background="Transparent">
  <ScrollViewer>
    <StackPanel Orientation="Vertical"
                Margin="12"
                Spacing="12">
      <TextBlock
        Style="{ThemeResource TitleTextBlockStyle}">
        Help and Support
      </TextBlock>
      <!-- The answers live here, in the app, because a headset user with a
           broken overlay cannot read a web page and may not have a second
           screen. Every fix below is one the user can carry out unaided. -->
      <Expander
        HorizontalAlignment="Stretch"
        HorizontalContentAlignment="Stretch"
        IsExpanded="True">
        <Expander.Header>
          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*">
            <FontIcon
              Grid.Column="0"
              Glyph="&#xE7BA;"
              FontFamily="{StaticResource SymbolThemeFontFamily}"
              Foreground="{ThemeResource AccentFillColorDefaultBrush}"
              Margin="0,0,12,0"
              VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
            <TextBlock
              Grid.Column="1"
              Text="The overlay doesn't appear in my game"
              Style="{StaticResource SubtitleTextBlockStyle}"
              VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
          </Grid>
        </Expander.Header>
        <StackPanel Orientation="Vertical" Spacing="8">
          <TextBlock Style="{ThemeResource BodyTextBlockStyle}" TextWrapping="Wrap">
            Work down this list; it is ordered by how often each one is the cause.
          </TextBlock>
          <TextBlock Style="{ThemeResource BodyTextBlockStyle}" TextWrapping="Wrap">
            1. <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Restart the game.</Run> VisorVR hooks
            into it through an OpenXR layer that is only loaded when the game
            starts. If VisorVR was not running when you launched the game, or
            was updated since, the game will not see it until you restart it.
          </TextBlock>
          <TextBlock Style="{ThemeResource BodyTextBlockStyle}" TextWrapping="Wrap">
            2. <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Check the layer is switched on</Run> —
            Settings → Virtual Reality → "OpenXR support for 64-bit games".
            VisorVR registers the layer for your Windows account each time it
            starts, so this needs no administrator rights.
          </TextBlock>
          <TextBlock Style="{ThemeResource BodyTextBlockStyle}" TextWrapping="Wrap">
            3. <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Check the panel is shown in VR</Run> —
            select it in the sidebar and confirm "Show this panel in VR" is on.
            A panel can be visible in this window and hidden in the headset.
          </TextBlock>
          <TextBlock Style="{ThemeResource BodyTextBlockStyle}" TextWrapping="Wrap">
            4. <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Check it isn't behind you.</Run> Use
            Edit in VR → Recenter to bring the panel back to where you are
            looking. This is the usual answer when it worked yesterday.
          </TextBlock>
          <TextBlock Style="{ThemeResource BodyTextBlockStyle}" TextWrapping="Wrap">
            5. <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Your game may not use OpenXR.</Run>
            Some titles default to OpenVR/SteamVR or to a vendor runtime; check
            the game's VR settings. See Compatibility below.
          </TextBlock>
        </StackPanel>
      </Expander>
      <Expander
        HorizontalAlignment="Stretch"
        HorizontalContentAlignment="Stretch">
        <Expander.Header>
          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*">
            <FontIcon
              Grid.Column="0"
              Glyph="&#xE8B9;"
              FontFamily="{StaticResource SymbolThemeFontFamily}"
              Foreground="{ThemeResource AccentFillColorDefaultBrush}"
              Margin="0,0,12,0"
              VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
            <TextBlock
              Grid.Column="1"
              Text="Placing and reading panels"
              Style="{StaticResource SubtitleTextBlockStyle}"
              VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
          </Grid>
        </Expander.Header>
        <StackPanel Orientation="Vertical" Spacing="8">
          <TextBlock Style="{ThemeResource BodyTextBlockStyle}" TextWrapping="Wrap">
            <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Move a panel:</Run> select it, then
            Edit in VR. With the headset on, grab the panel with the mouse to
            drag it, and follow the hints drawn on the panel for tilt and
            distance. It is saved as you go and survives a restart.
          </TextBlock>
          <TextBlock Style="{ThemeResource BodyTextBlockStyle}" TextWrapping="Wrap">
            <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Can't see the panel once you're in the
            headset?</Run> Press <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Recenter</Run>,
            next to Edit in VR. A panel keeps the position you last gave it, so
            it can easily be behind you, above you, or inside the car — and
            there is nothing to grab if you cannot see it. Recenter brings it
            back in front of wherever you are looking now, and is the first
            thing to try whenever a panel seems to have vanished.
          </TextBlock>
          <TextBlock Style="{ThemeResource BodyTextBlockStyle}" TextWrapping="Wrap">
            Bind <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Recenter VR</Run> to a button as
            well, so you can fix it mid-session without taking the headset off.
          </TextBlock>
          <TextBlock Style="{ThemeResource BodyTextBlockStyle}" TextWrapping="Wrap">
            <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">The text is fuzzy:</Run> raise
            Sharpness in the panel's settings. Size and Distance change how big
            a panel looks, but not how much detail it holds — only Sharpness
            does. It stops at 200% because past that the shared texture would
            exceed what the GPU can address.
          </TextBlock>
        </StackPanel>
      </Expander>
      <Expander
        HorizontalAlignment="Stretch"
        HorizontalContentAlignment="Stretch">
        <Expander.Header>
          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*">
            <FontIcon
              Grid.Column="0"
              Glyph="&#xE7FC;"
              FontFamily="{StaticResource SymbolThemeFontFamily}"
              Foreground="{ThemeResource AccentFillColorDefaultBrush}"
              Margin="0,0,12,0"
              VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
            <TextBlock
              Grid.Column="1"
              Text="Buttons: running VisorVR without leaving the headset"
              Style="{StaticResource SubtitleTextBlockStyle}"
              VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
          </Grid>
        </Expander.Header>
        <StackPanel Orientation="Vertical" Spacing="8">
          <TextBlock Style="{ThemeResource BodyTextBlockStyle}" TextWrapping="Wrap">
            Once you are in the headset and driving or flying, the keyboard is
            out of reach. Bind these to spare buttons on your wheel, HOTAS or
            controller before your first session — it is the difference between
            an overlay you use and one you fight.
          </TextBlock>
          <TextBlock Style="{ThemeResource BodyTextBlockStyle}" TextWrapping="Wrap">
            Go to <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Settings → Input</Run>, pick your
            device, then click a binding and press the button you want. The
            "Set up controller buttons" button in any panel's settings goes to
            the same place.
          </TextBlock>
          <TextBlock Style="{ThemeResource BodyTextBlockStyle}" TextWrapping="Wrap">
            <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Bind these three first:</Run>
          </TextBlock>
          <TextBlock Style="{ThemeResource BodyTextBlockStyle}" TextWrapping="Wrap">
            • <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Show/hide</Run> — takes the whole
            overlay off your view and brings it back. The one binding worth
            having even if you set up nothing else.
          </TextBlock>
          <TextBlock Style="{ThemeResource BodyTextBlockStyle}" TextWrapping="Wrap">
            • <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Recenter VR</Run> — pulls your panels
            back in front of you when they have ended up somewhere you cannot
            see, without unbuckling to reach the mouse.
          </TextBlock>
          <TextBlock Style="{ThemeResource BodyTextBlockStyle}" TextWrapping="Wrap">
            • <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Next panel</Run> / <Run
            FontWeight="SemiBold">Previous panel</Run> — cycles which panel you
            are looking at, so several panels can share one spot in front of
            you instead of crowding the cockpit.
          </TextBlock>
          <TextBlock Style="{ThemeResource BodyTextBlockStyle}" TextWrapping="Wrap">
            Also worth binding: <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Toggle VR edit
            mode</Run>, to reposition a panel from inside the headset without
            going back to this window; <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Next
            page</Run> / <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Previous page</Run> for
            panels holding multi-page documents; and <Run
            FontWeight="SemiBold">Increase</Run> / <Run
            FontWeight="SemiBold">Decrease brightness</Run> for night stints.
          </TextBlock>
        </StackPanel>
      </Expander>
      <Expander
        HorizontalAlignment="Stretch"
        HorizontalContentAlignment="Stretch">
        <Expander.Header>
          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*">
            <FontIcon
              Grid.Column="0"
              Glyph="&#xE774;"
              FontFamily="{StaticResource SymbolThemeFontFamily}"
              Foreground="{ThemeResource AccentFillColorDefaultBrush}"
              Margin="0,0,12,0"
              VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
            <TextBlock
              Grid.Column="1"
              Text="What to put in a panel"
              Style="{StaticResource SubtitleTextBlockStyle}"
              VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
          </Grid>
        </Expander.Header>
        <StackPanel Orientation="Vertical" Spacing="8">
          <TextBlock Style="{ThemeResource BodyTextBlockStyle}" TextWrapping="Wrap">
            <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">A web page.</Run> Anything with a URL:
            a timing screen, a chart, a checklist, a local page of your own.
            SimHub dashboards are supported directly — a dashboard tells
            VisorVR what size it wants to be, so the panel matches it.
          </TextBlock>
          <TextBlock Style="{ThemeResource BodyTextBlockStyle}" TextWrapping="Wrap">
            <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">A window from your desktop.</Run> Useful
            for an app with no web view. It keeps updating while the game has
            the screen.
          </TextBlock>
          <TextBlock Style="{ThemeResource BodyTextBlockStyle}" TextWrapping="Wrap">
            <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">A file or a folder.</Run> PDFs and
            images, paged through with a bound button — charts, plates,
            reference cards, a setup sheet.
          </TextBlock>
          <TextBlock Style="{ThemeResource BodyTextBlockStyle}" TextWrapping="Wrap">
            DCS World gets purpose-built panels — mission, briefing, aircraft
            kneeboard, radio log and terrain — from Settings → Panels.
          </TextBlock>
        </StackPanel>
      </Expander>
      <Expander
        HorizontalAlignment="Stretch"
        HorizontalContentAlignment="Stretch">
        <Expander.Header>
          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*">
            <FontIcon
              Grid.Column="0"
              Glyph="&#xE7F8;"
              FontFamily="{StaticResource SymbolThemeFontFamily}"
              Foreground="{ThemeResource AccentFillColorDefaultBrush}"
              Margin="0,0,12,0"
              VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
            <TextBlock
              Grid.Column="1"
              Text="Compatibility, files and privacy"
              Style="{StaticResource SubtitleTextBlockStyle}"
              VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
          </Grid>
        </Expander.Header>
        <StackPanel Orientation="Vertical" Spacing="8">
          <TextBlock Style="{ThemeResource BodyTextBlockStyle}" TextWrapping="Wrap">
            VisorVR draws into 64-bit OpenXR games — iRacing, DCS World,
            Microsoft Flight Simulator and most modern VR titles — whichever
            headset runtime you use. It does not touch flat-screen games.
          </TextBlock>
          <TextBlock Style="{ThemeResource BodyTextBlockStyle}" TextWrapping="Wrap">
            <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Your settings and logs</Run> live in
            %LOCALAPPDATA%\VisorVR. Nothing is sent anywhere: VisorVR has no
            telemetry, no account and no update service. A panel pointed at a
            web page reaches the internet exactly as a browser would.
          </TextBlock>
          <TextBlock Style="{ThemeResource BodyTextBlockStyle}" TextWrapping="Wrap">
            <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Uninstalling:</Run> delete the folder
            you unzipped, then that settings folder. VisorVR unregisters its
            OpenXR layer when it shuts down normally.
          </TextBlock>
        </StackPanel>
      </Expander>
      <StackPanel
        Orientation="Vertical"
        Spacing="4"
        Padding="8"
        Background="{ThemeResource CardBackgroundFillColorDefaultBrush}"
        BorderBrush="{ThemeResource CardStrokeColorDefaultBrush}"
        CornerRadius="4"
        BorderThickness="1">
        <HyperlinkButton
          NavigateUri="https://github.com/gbottlehead4-cmd/vr-overlay#readme">
          Setup and documentation
        </HyperlinkButton>
        <HyperlinkButton
          NavigateUri="https://github.com/gbottlehead4-cmd/vr-overlay/issues">
          Bug reports and feature requests
        </HyperlinkButton>
        <HyperlinkButton
          NavigateUri="https://discord.gg/DqPkr6vh4N">
          Discord — ask other users
        </HyperlinkButton>
        <TextBlock
          Foreground="{ThemeResource TextFillColorSecondary}"
          Style="{ThemeResource CaptionTextBlockStyle}"
          TextWrapping="Wrap">
          I make VisorVR for my own use, and share it in the hope others find it
          useful; I can't commit to support, bug fixes, or feature development.
          If something is broken, open an issue and attach the debugging
          information below — that is much more use to me than a description.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock
          Foreground="{ThemeResource TextFillColorSecondary}"
          Style="{ThemeResource CaptionTextBlockStyle}"
          TextWrapping="Wrap">
          This program is distributed in the hope that it will be useful,
          but WITHOUT ANY WARRANTY; without even the implied warranty of
          MERCHANTABILITY or FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE.  See the
          license information below for details.
        </TextBlock>
      </StackPanel>
      <TextBlock
        Style="{ThemeResource TitleTextBlockStyle}">
        About
      </TextBlock>
      <Expander
        HorizontalAlignment="Stretch"
        HorizontalContentAlignment="Stretch">
        <Expander.Header>
          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto,Auto">
            <FontIcon
              Grid.Column="0"
              Glyph="&#xE74C;"
              FontFamily="{StaticResource SymbolThemeFontFamily}"
              Margin="0,0,12,0"
              VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
            <TextBlock
              Grid.Column="1"
              Text="Version and Build"
              Style="{StaticResource SubtitleTextBlockStyle}"
              VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
            <Button
              Grid.Column="2"
              Content="Check for Updates"
              Click="OnCheckForUpdatesClick"
              VerticalAlignment="Center"
              Margin="0,0,12,0"/>
            <Button
              Grid.Column="3"
              Content="Copy"
              Click="OnCopyVersionDataClick"
              VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
          </Grid>
        </Expander.Header>
        <TextBlock Style="{ThemeResource BodyTextBlockStyle}"
                   x:Name="VersionText"
                   IsTextSelectionEnabled="true"/>
      </Expander>
      <Expander
        HorizontalAlignment="Stretch"
        HorizontalContentAlignment="Stretch">
        <Expander.Header>
          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto,Auto">
            <FontIcon
              Grid.Column="0"
              Glyph="&#xEB51;"
              FontFamily="{StaticResource SymbolThemeFontFamily}"
              Margin="0,0,12,0"
              VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
            <TextBlock
              Grid.Column="1"
              Text="Acknowledgements and Licenses"
              Style="{StaticResource SubtitleTextBlockStyle}"
              VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
          </Grid>
        </Expander.Header>
        <StackPanel
          Orientation="Vertical"
          Spacing="12"
          x:Name="Licenses">
          <TextBlock>
            No copyright files found; please file a bug if this is a
            released verison.
          </TextBlock>
        </StackPanel>
      </Expander>
      <Expander
        HorizontalAlignment="Stretch"
        HorizontalContentAlignment="Stretch">
        <Expander.Header>
          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto">
            <FontIcon
              Grid.Column="0"
              Glyph="&#xE81C;"
              FontFamily="{StaticResource SymbolThemeFontFamily}"
              Margin="0,0,12,0"
              VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
            <TextBlock
              Grid.Column="1"
              Text="Debugging Information"
              Style="{StaticResource SubtitleTextBlockStyle}"
              VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
          </Grid>
        </Expander.Header>
        <StackPanel Orientation="Vertical"
                    Spacing="8">
          <TextBlock
            Foreground="{ThemeResource TextFillColorPrimary}"
            Style="{ThemeResource BodyTextBlockStyle}">
            Debugging information and traces can include personal information,
            such as your Windows username, language settings, installation paths,
            graphics card make/model, VR headset make/model, OpenXR drivers or
            tools, and your VisorVR configuration. Sharing debugging information
            or traces may share this information with others.
          </TextBlock>
          <Button
            x:Name="AgreeButton"
            Click="OnAgreeClick"
            Content="I understand and agree"
            Style="{StaticResource AccentButtonStyle}"
            IsEnabled="{x:Bind AgreeButtonIsEnabled, Mode=OneWay}"/>
          <Button
            Click="OnExportClick"
            IsEnabled="{x:Bind AgreedToPrivacyWarning,Mode=OneWay}">
            <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*">
              <FontIcon
                Grid.Column="0"
                Glyph="&#xE792;"
                FontFamily="{StaticResource SymbolThemeFontFamily}"/>
              <TextBlock
                Margin="4,0,0,0"
                Grid.Column="1">
                Save as...
              </TextBlock>
            </Grid>
          </Button>
        </StackPanel>
      </Expander>
    </StackPanel>
  </ScrollViewer>
</Page>
